#! mathcas 2 notebook

#% title
# text

#% text
# `text(expr, [x, y])` is a graphics primitive: expr as a label centred at [x, y].

#% text
# `text(expr, [x, y], [ox, oy])` places the point [ox, oy] of the label's box at [x, y]: [-1, 0] its left end, [1, 0] its right end, [0, -1] its bottom, [0, 1] its top.

#% section
# Details

#% item
# A string is shown as text; between `$` signs it is math in a small TeX subset: `^` and `_` scripts, Greek letters and symbols by name, fractions and square roots, as in `"$\\alpha_1 \\le \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}$"` (a backslash is written twice in a string).

#% item
# `subscript(a, b)` and `superscript(a, b)` set b as an index or an exponent of a; any other expression is shown in its typeset form, single letters in italics.

#% item
# The label takes the current colour and the size of the last `font_size` directive, else the graphic's `font_size` option (10 points).

#% item
# The label itself is never evaluated further by `graphics`, so `text(pi, ...)` shows π.

#% section
# Examples

#% subsection
# Basic examples

#% text
# A word at the origin:

#% cell
graphics([circle(), text("centre", [0, 0])])
#% output
#= image("text.files/text-1.svgz")

#% text
# Math in a string:

#% cell
graphics(text("$E = m c^2$", [0, 0]))
#% output
#= image("text.files/text-2.svgz")

#% subsection
# Scope

#% text
# Expressions are typeset:

#% cell
graphics([text(x^2/2, [0, 0]), text(subscript(a, 1), [1, 0])])
#% output
#= image("text.files/text-3.svgz")

#% text
# Labels to the right of points:

#% cell
graphics([point([[0, 0], [1, 1]]), text("A", [0.05, 0], [-1, 0]), text("B", [1.05, 1], [-1, 0])])
#% output
#= image("text.files/text-4.svgz")

#% subsection
# Options

#% text
# A larger label in blue:

#% cell
graphics([blue, font_size(24), text("big", [0, 0])])
#% output
#= image("text.files/text-5.svgz")

#% subsection
# Possible issues

#% text
# The offset is a pair:

#% cell
text("a", [0, 0], 1)
#% output
#! failure(argument, text, 3)

#% section
# See also

#% text
# `font_size`, `graphics`, `plot`
